{"availability":"available","full":"Medialoco/sismo-la","name":"sismo-la","description":"A home seismograph on Arduino UNO Q. A small accelerometer records shakes; the USGS catalog says which ones were earthquakes; from those pairs the station learns magnitude and distance for this installation, then runs that model offline.","category":"Education","language":"Python","stars":2,"forks":1,"created":"2026-06-21T18:33:03Z","updated":"2026-10-10T14:59:54Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://medialoco.github.io/sismo-la/","topics":["2026","arduino","citizen-science","edge-ai","hackster","iot","open-data","seismology","usgs"],"demo":"https://medialoco.github.io/sismo-la/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-10T15:05:56.019Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-10T15:05:56.019Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-10T15:05:56.019Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-10T15:05:56.019Z","stars":2,"forks":1}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Sismo-LA is a small station in a house in Los Angeles County. It runs on USB-C and WiFi, for about $80. Its MEMS accelerometer — a chip from the same family as a phone's tilt sensor — measures vibrations in the ground and the building.","The USGS catalog is the official list of earthquakes in the region. A few minutes after each event it publishes magnitude, location, depth and exact origin time. Above about M1.5 here, it also lets one say that no other earthquake occurred. That second fact is what makes a silent station checkable.","The station compares what this box measured with what USGS reports. An empty detection list then has three readings: nothing was close enough; the station is blind; or it has stopped. Distinguishing those three is the point of the project. Matches, when they exist, would fit a model for this sensor on this shelf in this building. That model still has 0 of 8 points."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/Medialoco/sismo-la/blob/main/README.md",